From a1a2b60d891ec39a8df124835e6e0fe86eb2ecc9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: qiyinxi <qiyinxi@foxmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 04:39:24 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 40/57] os-win32: stop fully buffering stderr

"Fix log fragmentation in stdout/stderr" (Change-Id
Ibb89fb29c9f9624d47d349437146eee9fd019ed4) gave stderr a 4 KiB buffer
on Windows, where _IOLBF is the same as _IOFBF. Everything written to
stderr now stays in memory until 4 KiB accumulate or the process exits
normally: error_report() and warn_report(), plain fprintf(stderr)
calls in qemu, gfxstream and the media decoders, and FFmpeg's default
av_log() callback. abort() and the crashpad handler do not flush
stdio, so whatever was written to stderr just before a crash is lost,
and a reader on a pipe or a file sees nothing while the emulator runs.

The fragmentation that change fixed came from setbuf(stderr, NULL).
The UCRT gives stdout and stderr a temporary buffer for the duration
of a stdio call only if the stream has no buffer of its own, and
_IONBF counts as one, so every character went out in its own write.
stderr as the CRT sets it up gets the temporary buffer on every call,
console or not, and the buffer is flushed before the call returns:
one write per fprintf(), fputs() or fwrite(), and nothing kept.

Stop calling setvbuf() on stderr on Windows. Also skip the _IONBF
setvbuf() that qemu_set_log() applies when -d logs to stderr, which
would otherwise bring back the byte-at-a-time writes. stdout keeps its
4 KiB buffer.

Text written by one stdio call still arrives in one piece. A message
printed with several calls, such as an error_report(), may now arrive
in a few writes instead of one.

Test: emulator.exe -no-window ... > out.log 2> err.log; info_report()
      lines and a periodic fprintf(stderr) line appear in err.log
      while the emulator is running (before: only at exit)
Test: the console "crash" command: err.log still has every line that
      was written before the crash (before: the last 528 bytes, four
      lines including the info_report() ones, were lost)
Test: WriteFile() traced in a debugger: each fprintf(stderr) line is
      one write of the whole line, no 1-byte writes; the same with
      -qemu -d guest_errors,unimp and no -D (log on stderr)

Assisted-by: Claude Code:Claude-Opus-5.5
Change-Id: I9d4fde9201c4ce6d4830f58030645ba133f0bbc2
---
 os-win32.c | 15 ++++++++++++++-
 util/log.c | 10 ++++++++--
 2 files changed, 22 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/os-win32.c b/os-win32.c
index 12226ac56..d746ca9be 100644
--- a/os-win32.c
+++ b/os-win32.c
@@ -120,8 +120,21 @@ char *os_find_datadir(void)
 
 void os_set_line_buffering(void)
 {
+    /* The Windows CRT has no line buffering; _IOLBF means _IOFBF. */
     setvbuf(stdout, NULL, _IOFBF, 4096);
-    setvbuf(stderr, NULL, _IOFBF, 4096);
+
+    /*
+     * Leave stderr as the CRT sets it up. A stderr without a buffer of its
+     * own gets a temporary buffer for the duration of each stdio call,
+     * which is flushed before the call returns: every fprintf(), fputs()
+     * or fwrite() becomes one write, and nothing is held back.
+     *
+     * Any setvbuf() call on stderr is worse. _IONBF turns the temporary
+     * buffer off and writes one byte at a time. _IOFBF keeps up to 4 KiB
+     * of error output in memory: it shows up late, and is lost when the
+     * process aborts or crashes, because neither abort() nor the crash
+     * handler flushes stdio.
+     */
 }
 
 /*
diff --git a/util/log.c b/util/log.c
index 96f30dd21..838f8d5e4 100644
--- a/util/log.c
+++ b/util/log.c
@@ -86,8 +86,14 @@ void qemu_set_log(int log_flags)
             setvbuf(qemu_logfile, logfile_buf, _IOLBF, sizeof(logfile_buf));
         } else {
 #if defined(_WIN32)
-            /* Win32 doesn't support line-buffering, so use unbuffered output. */
-            setvbuf(qemu_logfile, NULL, _IONBF, 0);
+            /*
+             * Win32 doesn't support line-buffering, so use unbuffered
+             * output. stderr already writes once per call when left
+             * alone, see os_set_line_buffering().
+             */
+            if (qemu_logfile != stderr) {
+                setvbuf(qemu_logfile, NULL, _IONBF, 0);
+            }
 #else
             setvbuf(qemu_logfile, NULL, _IOLBF, 0);
 #endif
